FOREWORD
The ward profile contains the basic population counts at the ward level for every province. It is a series of publica-
tions produced directly from the 2011 National Population and Housing Census. It is a summary of the total popula-
tion by sex including additional information on the number of households for every ward.
The wards are the lowest level of government administration under the local level government and the ward profile 
is a direct response to the rapid growth in demand for social and economic data particularly at the lower level of 
planning.
For census purposes, PNG was divided into a number of geographical areas to facilitate both the conduct for census 
and the dissemination of census data to users. The hierarchical structure begins with the province, followed by the 
district, local level government, ward and census unit (village). The lowest unit for census data collection is the cen-
sus unit. The aggregated population counts at the census unit level make up the ward population counts as present-
ed in this series of publications.
Conducting a population census is never an easy task given the geographical setting in the country. Such mammoth 
task demands collaborative efforts from all sectors of the community.
NSO is indebted to the National Government, national departments and line agencies, development partners, provin-
cial administrations, community leaders and the general public in ensuring that the census was conducted success-
fully. The contribution of UNFPA in facilitating the engagement of a Data Processing Advisor is also acknowledged.
Roko Koloma        Hajily Kele
Acting National Statistician      Census Director

